# Purpose

The color-source theorem closed the structural coefficient:

```text
(1/2)(J_j - J_{b+sigma})^2.
```

The remaining orientation ambiguity is:

```text
sigma = +1  or  sigma = -1.
```

This note connects that ambiguity to the already-selected q79 retarded
predecessor convention.

# Existing Retarded Orientation

The selected CKM CP branch uses the dyadic lepton/lens quarter-turn:

```text
u_l = 16 in Z_64.
```

The selected quark branch is the retarded predecessor primitive survivor:

```text
q_64 = 15 = 16 - 1.
```

In local coordinate form:

```text
u_q = 16 - rho_q/kappa_q,
0 < rho_q/kappa_q < 2.
```

Thus the selected retarded nil-survivor direction is the predecessor direction.

# B_q Adjacent Role

The B_q cost is:

```text
D_q,sigma(i,j,b)^2 =
  (J_i - J_b)^2
  + (1/2)(J_j - J_{b+sigma})^2,
```

where

```text
b = -(i+j) mod 3.
```

The hidden adjacent role is not a new independent orientation datum.  It is the
same retarded survivor memory now acting on the finite family/color redundancy
cycle.

# Orientation-Lock Theorem

Assume:

1.  the selected q79 CP branch uses the retarded predecessor convention;

2.  the B_q hidden adjacent role is sourced by the same selected retarded
    nil/color survivor orientation;

3.  the family/color role cycle is written in the same orientation convention
    as the selected central-circle finite quotient.

Then:

```text
sigma = -1.
```

Therefore the selected-orientation B_q cost is:

```text
D_q,-(i,j,b)^2 =
  (J_i - J_b)^2
  + (1/2)(J_j - J_{b-1})^2.
```

# Proof

By assumption 1, the selected retarded survivor direction maps a reference
role to its predecessor.  In the dyadic CP coordinate this is:

```text
16 -> 15 = 16 - 1.
```

By assumption 2, the hidden adjacent role in B_q is not an independent
successor/predecessor choice; it is the same retarded survivor direction
applied to the finite family/color redundancy role.  Therefore:

```text
b -> b - 1.
```

By assumption 3, this predecessor is represented as `sigma=-1` in the B_q
formula.

Thus:

```text
sigma = -1.
```

